WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.724
Film Courage: What would you say those&nbsp;
two web series did for your career?

00:00:02.724 --> 00:00:07.440
Sujata Day, Actor, Writer/Director: The Guild&nbsp;
and The Awkward Black Girl put me on the map I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.860
believe in terms of exposure even at that time I&nbsp;
had just done season two of Awkward Black Girl I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:19.440
had just done season six of The Guild and then I&nbsp;
got a call from my commercial agent. At this point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.640
I did not have a theatrical agent and they said&nbsp;
“Hey the casting director of Arrested Development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:34.020
is coming back and the casting director wants to&nbsp;
offer you a small role.” I said “What? How did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:41.460
they even know about me? I'm not auditioning&nbsp;
for TV shows or films right now?” They said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.760
“They had been watching web series and they&nbsp;
just discovered me so that was getting to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:58.080
an episode of Arrested Development was&nbsp;
really really special and and being on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:05.280
proper television set with hair and&nbsp;
makeup and craft services and them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:15.300
picking up my outfit and being on set with David&nbsp;
Cross and Alia Shawkat that was just an experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.920
that I will never forget because I wasn't&nbsp;
expecting it at the time would you say you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.040
here now because of those two series has your life&nbsp;
sort of your career the trajectory of it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.440
of those series the timing was right that it just&nbsp;
brought other great things into your life other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:40.740
roles oh yeah I credit awkward black girl with&nbsp;
pretty much my entire career and and the path that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:47.520
I personally chose to take which is creating my&nbsp;
own content and putting my own stuff out there and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.940
taking risks and not sitting by the phone waiting&nbsp;
for it to ring waiting for my agent to call me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:03.480
almost with those calls when they do come are&nbsp;
presents because I'm continuing to make my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.760
content and then when my agent does call with&nbsp;
something I'm oh that's cool that's surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:17.460
and exciting so so I love that about my life and&nbsp;
definitely I credit everything too Awkward Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:24.600
Girl you talked about risk earlier waiting by&nbsp;
the phone and now that you can take chances and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.940
a risk to create your own work but even from a&nbsp;
young age it sounds with the spelling bees for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:35.580
however long that lasted that's a risk that was&nbsp;
definitely a risk especially at that time I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:44.340
a shy introverted kid so to get up in front of an&nbsp;
audience I remember that moment because when when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.740
you're in class of eight and you're just doing the&nbsp;
spelling bee there's no audience it's just you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.360
your word right or wrong and if you get it wrong&nbsp;
you just sit and you become the audience and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:02.160
was around my friends so I do remember going to&nbsp;
the regionals and seeing that huge audience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:09.360
that probably scared me off as well so what I'm&nbsp;
just making excuses for spelling the word radish

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:17.100
but anyway so it because I see you as that&nbsp;
you have a lot of you wouldn't be fearful of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:24.540
of challenges but it seems you yeah I'm not&nbsp;
fearful of challenges now I will say I said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:32.220
I was a shy introverted kid and then in seventh&nbsp;
grade when I switched to public school and had all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:38.580
these friends I looked in the mirror and I said to&nbsp;
myself I cannot be shy and introverted anymore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:43.800
then I just switched and then I was getting kicked&nbsp;
out of math class because I was talking too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:50.220
there was a discussion on Twitter a few weeks back&nbsp;
about the film Community the writing community and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.440
the wrong way and the right way to do something&nbsp;
and I just wondered what your thoughts are on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:02.400
is there a wrong way to make a movie is there a&nbsp;
right way you talked about over the course of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:09.420
pandemic you made watching movies sort of your&nbsp;
film school but there is a dogma of this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.820
right way to do something the right way to enter a&nbsp;
scene to end a scene do you feel that or everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:21.840
should have their own unique style I don't feel&nbsp;
there's a right or a wrong way I think there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:28.860
greats that have come before us that we can learn&nbsp;
a lot from and they all have their own Styles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:36.060
if you're watching something David Lynch and Twin&nbsp;
Peaks you notice that he he tends to break the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:43.440
rules and and go into melodrama which was the best&nbsp;
part about Twin Peaks I just loved it so much but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:52.860
then you then you watch something Good Fellas and&nbsp;
that feels what a film buff would say is the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.860 --> 00:05:00.000
way to make a movie but but I think I I just&nbsp;
love all kinds of movies and especially if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.680
watch International films if you watch something&nbsp;
that I'm really inspired by is sethiji thrai who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:16.920
is a Bengali filmmaker I'm also Bengali and he&nbsp;
won an honorary Oscar in the 80s or the 90s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.980
he what he did was he kind of broke off well he&nbsp;
didn't want to make Bollywood type movies which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:29.040
is what Indian India was known for at the time&nbsp;
and he just went into the villages and started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:35.160
focusing on women in The Villages and and&nbsp;
he would write these amazing stories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:40.980
just regular women and what they were going&nbsp;
through and and it wasn't about being rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:48.000
and it wasn't about musical dance numbers and&nbsp;
and it was just a quiet story and so what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:55.680
believe is there's not a right or wrong way to do&nbsp;
or create art and I think what's exciting to me is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.800
when you do get out of those boxes and and we&nbsp;
were talking about risk when you take a risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.800
and and maybe it doesn't pan out the way that&nbsp;
you wanted to in this project then you don't do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:13.800
it again in the next one you take a different&nbsp;
kind of risk and I love when projects surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:19.560
me I love it when they go a certain way or do a&nbsp;
certain thing that I'm not expecting and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:27.360
what's exciting to me about art which movies and&nbsp;
TV shows fall into as well has anyone said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.780
to you this should be more this if you if you do&nbsp;
it more this way it'll have more marketability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:41.280
I believe that happens more in the screenwriting&nbsp;
process and not really in the filmmaking process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:50.460
I I get notes from my scripts and my pitches&nbsp;
and honestly most of the time the notes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:57.540
make the project better so it allows me to&nbsp;
think about the project in a different way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.580
that I may not have seen before and and that's&nbsp;
what's really exciting sometimes what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:09.600
is you'll go through a circle of notes taking you&nbsp;
back to that same place where you started but now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:17.040
you're more clear about why you started with&nbsp;
that story so it's I appreciate notes I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:25.920
getting very hard critical notes on my work&nbsp;
and and I won't necessarily take 100 of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:35.160
notes but each note allows me to think about&nbsp;
the scene or my characters or the work in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:42.240
different way so I'm okay where is this now&nbsp;
coming from what is the person not attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:48.300
onto what what are they not getting in&nbsp;
this part and that's where the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.700
note is coming from so getting notes to me is&nbsp;
very very awesome because generally every time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:58.020
I've gotten notes and I've done the rewrite it's&nbsp;
a better script than what I had written before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:05.040
did anybody give you notes with definition please&nbsp;
can you make it go more in this direction and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.900
we'll have more marketability for it because&nbsp;
it's really just a story about coming being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.500
young person whether you want to leave the&nbsp;
nest or stay in your small town it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.780
have to be Bollywood it was just an experience&nbsp;
that happened to be different cultures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.940
all came together and knew each other from high&nbsp;
school yeah I got the I got one note that I got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:32.100
I believe it was during the script process&nbsp;
was someone needs to die it needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:42.300
in this climax moment one of the family members&nbsp;
needs to die and I was I don't think so I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:49.980
think that needs to happen I'm really just&nbsp;
trying to make it a slice of life and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:57.000
could be any family it just happens to be a South&nbsp;
Asian American family in the center of it all and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:05.580
that's what was exciting to me just to show Indian&nbsp;
Americans as normal people just everyone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.340
I think mostly when I when I soft pitched it&nbsp;
